Hm... WebKit also applies a bunch of other styles to `button`:
```css
input, textarea, keygen, select, button {
    margin: 0__qem;
#if !(defined(WTF_PLATFORM_IOS_FAMILY) && WTF_PLATFORM_IOS_FAMILY); // i.e. only in iOS
    font: -webkit-small-control;
#endif
    color: initial;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    word-spacing: normal;
    line-height: normal;
    text-transform: none;
    text-indent: 0;
    text-shadow: none;
    display: inline-block;
    text-align: start;
}
```

For clarity, the proposal in CSS is

```
button, input, select, textarea {
  letter-spacing: initial;
  word-spacing: initial;
  line-height: initial;
  text-transform: initial;
  text-indent: initial;
  text-shadow: initial;
}
input, select, textarea, marquee {
  text-align: initial;
}
table {
  text-indent: initial;
}
```

@MatsPalmgren , do you still disagree with this? Although `button` isn't a replaced element, it still has various "magic" behaviors, and these properties being reset can be explained by that being what legacy implementations do. (Similarly for `table` and `text-indent`.)
